// Mortuary operations: the register of bodies in the mortuary's care, the
// storage spaces and where each body has been, the belongings and their chain
// of custody, postmortem requests and the authorities behind them, the
// release checks and the release itself, and the occupancy board.
//
// Owning context : mortuary  (Master Engineering Registry)
// Owning service : MortuaryService
// Trace          : SRS-MORT-001 … SRS-MORT-008
//
// Four shapes in this contract are deliberate.
//
// OpenCase takes a source and refuses anything in between. An in-hospital
// death names its encounter; a body brought in says who brought it and names
// no encounter here. There is no field that means "linked, probably", because
// "which death is this?" is the question a family, a registrar and a coroner
// all ask first, and a case that answers it with an identifier somebody typed
// is a case that can point at the wrong person.
//
// Release runs every check and there is no field that skips one. A
// medico-legal case and an unidentified body are released on a named
// authority's clearance with that authority's own reference, and no policy
// setting turns that off — SRS-MORT-007's "cannot bypass required
// authorization" is not a configuration option. The other checks are policy:
// a deployment decides whether it holds a body for the death certificate,
// whether it releases on a presumed identification, and whether the
// belongings must be settled first. GetReleaseChecks returns the whole list
// at once, because a mortuary told "no identity check" will do the identity
// check and come back to be told "no death certificate".
//
// The cause of death and the medico-legal reference are absent from every
// list and from every event. GetCase carries them only for a caller holding
// mort.sensitive.read, and that read is audited each time; ListCases and the
// occupancy board have no field for them at all. SRS-MORT-003's acceptance is
// that unauthorised reads are denied and audited, and a board that could show
// a cause of death is one that will, on a screen in a corridor.
//
// Belongings move by handover and never by edit. A handover names the
// recipient, the document that identified them, a second member of staff and
// a signature reference; an item an authority has taken is retained rather
// than handed over, because the family has not got it and telling them
// otherwise would be untrue.
//
